import{_ as n,C as r,o,c as s,a2 as a,E as i}from"./chunks/framework.BAQ5ZYJQ.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"What errand is","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/what-is-errand.md","filePath":"guide/what-is-errand.md","lastUpdated":1792187838000}'),d={name:"guide/what-is-errand.md"};function h(c,e,l,u,p,m){const t=r("Term");return o(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a('<h1 id="what-errand-is" tabindex="-1">What errand is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-errand-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What errand is&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p><strong>errand runs your work in any environment — a conda or uv env, a container, another machine over ssh, a Slurm allocation, any stack of them — building and updating it from what it declares, and syncing your files there and back.</strong></p></blockquote><p>An <em>errand</em> is a trip made on someone&#39;s behalf: go there, do the thing, come back with what it produced. You say what to run and where it may run; errand makes sure that place is ready, goes in — through ssh, a Slurm allocation, a container, whatever the way is — runs it, in several places at once if you ask, and brings back what you asked for.</p><p>What you run is <strong>yours</strong>: what follows <code>errand run</code> is what you would have typed — <code>make check</code>, <code>python train.py</code>, <code>./bench</code>, <code>pytest</code>. errand does not need to know what language it is in.</p><h2 id="environments-built-and-kept-current" tabindex="-1">Environments, built and kept current <a class="header-anchor" href="#environments-built-and-kept-current" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Environments, built and kept current&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is the part that is hard to do by hand and tedious to do twice. An environment is a <strong>stack of layers</strong>: micromamba, conda, uv, a venv, Nix or Guix, <code>module load</code>, a Docker, Podman or Apptainer container, another machine over ssh, a Slurm or OAR allocation, a Windows virtual machine — in any combination. Read it outside in: <em>that machine, an allocation on it, a container, your command.</em></p>',6)),i(t,{scene:"stack",caption:"errand run --env cluster train.py: three layers, declared once, crossed by one flag."}),e[1]||(e[1]=a(`<p>The shell script that does <code>ssh</code>, then <code>sbatch</code>, then <code>apptainer exec</code>, then rsyncs the results back — and that nobody else can run — is replaced by three lines in a file. And each line is more than a way in:</p><ul><li><strong>It is built from what it declares.</strong> A layer says what it needs — a Python version, a <code>requirements.txt</code>, a channel list, a container recipe, a pip list — and the first run that needs it creates it, installs into it, builds the image.</li><li><strong>It is kept current.</strong> Before every run errand compares a fingerprint of the declaration with what it last built, and brings the place up to date when they have drifted: a new pip spec, an edited recipe. What already exists the first time errand meets it is adopted as it is — an environment somebody has been working in for months is never the collateral of a first command.</li><li><strong>Files cross with the work.</strong> Over <code>Ssh</code> the project is rsynced there before the command runs; <code>--pull DIR</code> brings a directory back afterwards, <code>-o</code> gives each run an output directory over there and fetches it, and <code>also</code> pushes the sibling checkouts the work needs.</li><li><strong>Several at once.</strong> <code>--env local,cluster</code> is two runs; a tag picks every environment that has it, and one environment can cover several values of a tag.</li></ul><p>→ <a href="./environments">Environments</a>, <a href="./upkeep">Keeping them current</a>, <a href="./tags">Tags</a>, <a href="./remote">Running elsewhere</a></p><h2 id="what-you-also-get" tabindex="-1">What you also get <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-you-also-get" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What you also get&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Nothing to declare for any of it:</p><ul><li><strong>The machine to yourself when it matters.</strong> A queue per host, outside the process, shared by every errand on the machine: <code>errand run -x ./bench</code> waits for what is running and holds everything else back. A timing taken while something else was running is not a timing. → <a href="./machine">Sharing the machine</a></li><li><strong>Let it run.</strong> <code>--batch</code> launches the work and gives the shell back; <code>errand status</code> and <code>errand watch</code> say how it is going. → <a href="./detached">Detached runs</a></li><li><strong>A record of every run</strong> — what ran, where, when, how it ended, what it said — kept in errand&#39;s own state, <code>.errand/</code>. With <code>-o</code>, a directory per run whose path is computed from the work, the place and the date, so that comparison between dates and machines falls out of the tree. → <a href="./output">Where the output goes</a></li><li><strong>One screen for all of it</strong>: <code>errand tui</code>. → <a href="./tui">The screen</a></li></ul><h2 id="suites-and-errand-s-own-entries" tabindex="-1">Suites, and errand&#39;s own entries <a class="header-anchor" href="#suites-and-errand-s-own-entries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Suites, and errand&#39;s own entries&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A plain <code>errand run tests/</code> is a directory, not a command. <code>errand run &#39;tests/**::*&#39;</code> is: the <code>::</code> hands the files to an <strong>entry runner</strong> — pytest, Catch2, cargo — which collects one entry per test and records each. A suite keeps everything that makes it itself: <code>pytest tests/</code> still works exactly as before, and a <code>@pytest.mark.slow</code> becomes an entry tag that <code>-e &#39;!slow&#39;</code> filters on.</p><p>For work that has no framework of its own — a number to follow, a picture to look at — errand has a small declaration that sits beside the code, <code>if track( &quot;cost&quot; ):</code>. It is one more way to give errand something to run, not the way in.</p><p>→ <a href="./running">Running it</a>, <a href="./runners">Runners</a>, <a href="./declaring-work">Writing entries</a></p><h2 id="what-it-is-not" tabindex="-1">What it is not <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-is-not" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What it is not&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>It is not a build system.</strong> errand compiles nothing itself. A runner that needs a build runs the command you already build with, once, before any of its entries. Your compiler, your flags and your layout stay yours.</p><p><strong>It is not a replacement for your test runner.</strong> What a suite gains is everything that happens <em>around</em> the run.</p><p><strong>It is not a scheduler.</strong> Inside a Slurm, PBS, OAR or LSF allocation it does not queue at all — the scheduler has already decided what this process may have. Outside one, <code>Slurm</code> is a layer that <em>submits</em> to the scheduler for you.</p><p><strong>It does not decide what may be compared to what.</strong> A matrix that spans a laptop and a cluster partition will produce numbers that are not comparable, and errand will not stop you. It records where each number came from; the judgement is yours.</p><h2 id="the-shape-of-a-project" tabindex="-1">The shape of a project <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-shape-of-a-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The shape of a project&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Nothing is required: <code>errand run make</code> works in an empty directory, in the interpreter and on the machine you started it from. Once you want to say <em>where</em> things run, files at the root say it — errand reads every <code>errand-*.py</code>:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>errand-envs.py         the environments — versioned when it names no machine   ← errand init</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>errand-project.py      runners, what a bare \`errand run\` runs, settings        ← errand init</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>errand-vars.py         what only this machine knows — never versioned</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>.errand/               errand&#39;s own record of what it did</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>runs/                  the output directories you asked for with -o</span></span></code></pre></div><p>→ <a href="./configuration">Configuration</a></p><h2 id="next" tabindex="-1">Next <a class="header-anchor" href="#next" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Next&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="./installing">Installing</a> · <a href="./start">Getting started</a> · or look at the layers at work in <a href="/errand/tutorials/environments">Four environments</a>.</p>`,21))])}const w=n(d,[["render",h]]);export{y as __pageData,w as default};
